 $$1+2+\ldots+(i-1)+(i-1)+(i+1)+(i+2)+\ldots+(j-1)+(j-1)+(j+1)+(j+2)+\ldots+n $$ 
 how many terms are there here I think we should do the sum of the first  $n-2$  terms? 

Answer: That's trivial. You have a Gaussian sum minus two units. It's just a matter of interpreting and rewriting the terms. Note that 
 \begin{align}
&1 + \cdots + (i-1) + (\color{red}{i}-\color{red}{1}) + (i+1) + \cdots + (j-1)+(\color{blue}{j}-\color{blue}{1})+ (j+1) + \cdots + n\\
&= \left[1 + \cdots + (i-1) + \color{red}{i} + (i+1) + \cdots + (j-1)+\color{blue}{j}+ (j+1) + \cdots + n\right] -\color{red}{1}-\color{blue}{1}\\
&= \left[\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\right]-2
\end{align} 
