{"input_text": "Question: As the title shows, is there a way to calculate the eigenvalues of  $A\equiv \vec x\vec x^T+\vec y \vec y^T$ , where  $\vec x$  and  $\vec y$  are two linearly independent vectors in  $\mathbb{R}^n$ (don't have to be unit vector). 
 Here're some of my thoughts. We can see that  $A$  is of rank 2 since it can be seen as a map from  $\mathbb{R}^n$  to  $\mathbb{R}^n$  and there are only two linearly independent vectors in its range. So we may set the eigenvectors of  $A$  as  $a\vec x+b\vec y$ , where  $a$  and  $b$  are two real numbers, then we have chances to get the eigenvalues by solving  $$A(a\vec x+b\vec y) =\lambda (a\vec x+b\vec y)\tag{1}.$$ 
 For example, if  $\vec{x}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left( \begin{array}{c}
 1\\
 1\\
\end{array} \right) , \vec{y}=\left( \begin{array}{c}
 1\\
 0\\
\end{array} \right) $ , we can solve eq(1) to get the eigenvalues  $1\pm \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}$ . But eq(1) only have two equations while with 3 parameters  $a,b,\lambda$ . 
 So my question is : is there a way to calculate the eigenvalues of  $xx^T+yy^T$ , such as a formula related to  $\vec x$  and  $\vec y$  that I don't know? 

Answer: $
\def\a{\alpha}\def\b{\beta}\def\g{\gamma}\def\l{\lambda}
\def\c#1{\color{red}{#1}}
\def\m#1{\left[\begin{array}{r}#1\end{array}\right]}
$ Define the variables
 $$\eqalign{
Z &= \m{x&y},\qquad &c=\m{a\\b} \\
A &= ZZ^T,\qquad &w=Zc \;=\;  ax+by \\
}$$ 
Consider the eigenvalue equation
 $$\eqalign{
Z^TZc &= \l c \\
}$$ 
Multiplying by  $Z$  recovers the original eigenvalue equation
 $$\eqalign{
ZZ^T\c{Zc} &= \l\c{Zc} \\
A\c{w} &= \l\c{w} \\
}$$ 
If we define further variables 
 $$\eqalign{
Z^TZ &= \m{\a&\g\\ \g&\b},\quad
\a=x^Tx,\quad\b=y^Ty,\quad\g=x^Ty \\
}$$ 
Then the eigenvalues of  $Z^TZ$  can be calculated
as the roots of a simple quadratic equation
 $$\eqalign{
&\det(Z^TZ-\l I) 
  \;=\; \l^2-(\a+\b)\l+(\a\b-\g^2) \;=\; 0 \\
&\l_{\pm} = \frac{(\a+\b)\pm\sqrt{(\a-\b)^2+4\g^2}}{2} \\
}$$ 
